For a topic model fitted by \code{\link[=hg_topics]{hg_topics()}} (\code{topics}), two topics are
related when the same documents draw on both. Without \code{threshold}, the
weight is the correlation of the two topics' shares over the documents,
and the pairs with a correlation above \code{cutoff} are kept, the "simple"
topic correlation of \code{stm::topicCorr()} (Roberts et al. 2019). With
\code{threshold}, a topic counts as present in a document when its share is at
least \code{threshold}, and the weight of a pair is the number of documents in
which both are present (Abuhay et al. 2017; Cassi et al. 2017).

A count network, from a partition or from a thresholded topic model, can
be normalised by the similarity measures of bibnets' \code{normalize()}, with
each topic's total on the diagonal (its documents with the word, or its
documents with the topic present). With \code{D_i} the diagonal entry of topic
